SIGNAL = r"""{\sffamily\bfseries\small\color{Deep}In 493\,388 Shenzhen adults with 1.03 million examination intervals, each interquartile rise in 365-day PM$_{2.5}$ (5.0\,$\mu$g\,m$^{-3}$) carried a hazard ratio of 1.054 (95\,\% CI 1.030--1.078) for forward progression of cardiovascular--kidney--metabolic syndrome --- but the PM$_{2.5}$ estimate moved with model specification while the O$_3$ estimate did not.}\\[1.4mm]
\footnotesize
Shenzhen Center for Chronic Disease Control, \textit{Ecotoxicology and Environmental Safety}. Multistate Cox models over 162\,083 forward transitions, exposures re-set at each interval: PM$_{2.5}$ HR 1.073 (1.030--1.118) into stages 1--2 and 1.055 (1.027--1.085) into stages 3--4; O$_3$ per IQR (7.4\,$\mu$g\,m$^{-3}$) 1.151 (1.138--1.165) overall and consistent in six-pollutant and multipollutant specifications.
\textbf{Where it is weak:} progression is defined by consecutive health examinations, so visit frequency --- itself tied to health-seeking and to symptoms --- shapes when an event is recorded; the exposure product is not named in the abstract; and the authors themselves report PM$_{2.5}$ as the more specification-sensitive of the two pollutants.
\textbf{Why it matters here:} the contrast carrying this HR is 5\,$\mu$g\,m$^{-3}$, which is within the uncorrected error of most optical low-cost sensors. A cohort this size can absorb that error; a sensor-based cohort of a few hundred people cannot, which is the argument for co-location and humidity correction before any health model is fitted."""

